  "overview": "The Pico is the Raspberry Pi Foundation's first microcontroller board (not a mini Linux computer like the rest of the Pi lineup) built around their own RP2040 chip. Its standout feature is flexibility: almost any GPIO pin can be reassigned to I2C, SPI, UART, or PWM through a programmable pin-mux, instead of those functions being hard-wired to specific pins like on classic AVR Arduinos. It also has two unusual extras: 'PIO' state machines that can bit-bang custom protocols in hardware, and dual cores that can run independent code. No WiFi/Bluetooth on the base Pico (the Pico W variant adds that).",
  "simpleSummary": "A cheap, fast microcontroller board from the makers of the Raspberry Pi computer.",
  "specifications": {
    "microcontroller": "RP2040, dual-core Arm Cortex-M0+, 133 MHz",
    "operatingVoltage": "3.3V",
    "inputVoltageRecommended": "5V via USB (VBUS) or 1.8-5.5V via VSYS",
    "flashMemory": "2 MB (external QSPI flash)",
    "sram": "264 KB",
    "digitalIOPins": 26,
    "adc": "3 usable analog inputs (12-bit), plus an internal temperature sensor channel",
    "connector": "Micro-USB"
  },
